.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DTFL_SUMMARY_RESULTS_IN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_summary_results_in ,
.Nm dtfl_summary_followed_by ,
.Nm dtfl_summary_less_equal ,
.Nm dtfl_summary_default ,
.Nm dtfl_coord_from_i64 ,
.Nm dtfl_coord_to_i64 ,
.Nm dtfl_time_format
.Nd path summaries, coordinate encoding and formatting
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libdtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/time.h
.Ft bool
.Fn dtfl_summary_results_in "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "const uint64_t *summary" "const uint64_t *time" "uint64_t *out"
.Ft bool
.Fn dtfl_summary_followed_by "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "const uint64_t *a" "const uint64_t *b" "uint64_t *out"
.Ft bool
.Fn dtfl_summary_less_equal "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "const uint64_t *a" "const uint64_t *b"
.Ft void
.Fn dtfl_summary_default "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "uint64_t *out"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn dtfl_coord_from_i64 "int64_t v"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn dtfl_coord_to_i64 "uint64_t w"
.Ft int
.Fn dtfl_time_format "const dtfl_time_class *tc" "const uint64_t *t" "char *buf" "size_t len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A path summary says how a time changes along a dataflow path, for
example around a loop.
It is an array of
.Fn dtfl_summary_depth tc
words:
.Bl -tag -width POINTSTAMP
.It unit, AltNeu
no words; the summary does nothing.
.It total
one increment, encoded as the coordinate is.
.It product, lex
the first child's words, then the second's.
.It PointStamp
.Fa max_len
+ 1 words.
Word 0 is
.Em retain ,
the number of leading coordinates kept, or
.Dv UINT64_MAX
to keep all.
The rest are increments, one per coordinate, zero padded.
.El
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_summary_results_in
applies
.Fa summary
to
.Fa time
and stores the result in
.Fa out .
Each coordinate adds its increment with an overflow check at the
coordinate's declared width, so a
.Vt u32
time overflows at 2^32 - 1 even though it is stored in 64 bits.
A PointStamp summary first resets coordinates past
.Em retain
to the minimum.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_summary_followed_by
composes
.Fa a
then
.Fa b
into
.Fa out .
Applying the result is the same as applying
.Fa a
and then
.Fa b ,
whenever both ways produce a time.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_summary_less_equal
is the order on summaries that progress tracking keeps antichains
under.
For PointStamp summaries it requires equal
.Em retain
and then compares the increments.
.Fn dtfl_summary_default
stores the identity summary.
.Pp
Signed coordinates are stored with the sign bit flipped,
.Li (uint64_t)v ^ (1 << 63) ,
so that unsigned comparison of stored words is signed comparison.
.Fn dtfl_coord_from_i64
encodes a value and
.Fn dtfl_coord_to_i64
decodes one.
A narrower signed coordinate stores its value sign-extended to 64
bits first.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_time_format
writes a time into
.Fa buf
as Rust's
.Li {:?}
would, for example
.Li (3, 7)
or
.Li PointStamp { vector: [1, 5] } ,
except that a Duration prints as seconds with nine decimals and an
.Li s .
It truncates as
.Xr snprintf 3
does, and
.Fa buf
may be
.Dv NULL
when
.Fa len
is 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_summary_results_in
and
.Fn dtfl_summary_followed_by
return false where Rust returns
.Dv None ,
on overflow, and leave
.Fa out
unchanged then.
.Fa out
may be the same array as an input.
.Fn dtfl_time_format
returns the length of the full text, as
.Xr snprintf 3 .
.Sh ERRORS
None.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
All of these are pure functions of their arguments, safe to call from
any thread.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A loop that adds one to a
.Vt u32
time drops the time once it would pass the maximum:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint64_t s = 1, t = UINT32_MAX, out;

if (!dtfl_summary_results_in(tc, &s, &t, &out))
	/* the feedback edge discards it */;
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_time_class 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_time_cmp 3
.Sh HISTORY
These are timely-dataflow's
.Vt PathSummary
(timely/src/progress/timestamp.rs) and differential-dataflow's
.Vt PointStampSummary
(differential-dataflow/src/dynamic/pointstamp.rs), at the pins.
Upstream compares PointStamp summaries that differ only by trailing
zero increments as different; here they are equal, because they act
identically.
